{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"primitives-vs-references","title":"Primitives vs References","group":"Getting Started","order":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Primitives are copied by value; objects are shared by reference. Almost every “why did my data change?” bug comes from this.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Copying a primitive</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>let a = 10;\nlet b = a;     // b gets its own copy of the value\nb = 20;\nconsole.log(a, b);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 20</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Copying an object reference</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const original = { score: 10 };\nconst alias = original;   // copies the *reference*, not the object\nalias.score = 99;\nconsole.log(original.score);\nconsole.log(original === alias);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>99\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Primitives</th><th>Objects (incl. arrays, functions)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stored as</td><td>The value itself</td><td>A reference to a heap object</td></tr><tr><td>Assignment copies</td><td>The value</td><td>The reference</td></tr><tr><td><code>===</code> compares</td><td>Values</td><td>Identity (same object?)</td></tr><tr><td>Mutable?</td><td>No — immutable</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Equality compares identity for objects</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(\"abc\" === \"abc\");\nconsole.log({ a: 1 } === { a: 1 });\nconsole.log([1, 2] === [1, 2]);\nconst arr = [1, 2];\nconsole.log(arr === arr);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true\nfalse\nfalse\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i>Passing to functions: always by value (of the reference)</h3>\n<p>JavaScript is <strong>pass-by-value</strong>. For objects the <em>value</em> being passed is a reference — so a function can mutate the caller's object, but reassigning the parameter never affects the caller.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function mutate(obj) {\n  obj.touched = true;   // changes the shared object\n}\nfunction reassign(obj) {\n  obj = { replaced: true };  // only rebinds the local parameter\n}\nconst data = {};\nmutate(data);\nreassign(data);\nconsole.log(data);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{ touched: true }</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Primitives are immutable</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const s = \"hello\";\nconst upper = s.toUpperCase();   // returns a NEW string\nconsole.log(s, upper);\nconst t = \"cat\";\ntry {\n  t[0] = \"b\";                    // strings can't change in place\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.name + \": \" + e.message);\n}\nconsole.log(t);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hello HELLO\nTypeError: Cannot assign to read only property '0' of string 'cat'\ncat</code></pre></figure>\n<p>In strict mode (every ES module) the assignment throws; in a sloppy script it is <em>silently ignored</em>. Either way the string never changes.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Autoboxing</div><p>Methods on primitives work because of <strong>autoboxing</strong>: <code>\"hi\".length</code> briefly wraps the string in a <code>String</code> object, reads the property, and discards the wrapper.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>To copy an object, use spread (<code>{...obj}</code>, shallow) or <code>structuredClone(obj)</code> (deep) — see <em>Immutability &amp; Copying</em>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is JavaScript pass-by-reference?</summary><div><p>No. It is always pass-by-value; for objects the value is a reference (sometimes called <em>call by sharing</em>). Mutating through the parameter is visible to the caller, but reassigning the parameter is not.</p></div></details></section>"}